      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Number: R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02, revision 3\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3800938\n- Page total: 9\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 11 September 2024\n- OIML issuer id: NL1 (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- Emerson Process Management Flow B.V., Neostraat 1, 6718 WX Ede, The Netherlands (Applicant and Manufacturer)\n\nManufacturers:\n- Same: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: \"Measurement transducer\" — the identification says \"A measurement transducer\". Categories listed include \"Measuring system\", etc. The type is a Coriolis mass flow measurement transducer. I'll use \"Measurement transducer\" — hmm, the schema says category examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... I'll use \"Measurement transducer\" as that's what's on the cert.\n- type_designations: CMFxxx (with footnote 1), CMFHCx (with footnote 1). Footnote: [1] With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\n\nThe type designations contain footnote markers `<sup>[1]</sup>`. I should strip footnote markers from the type names? Rule 1 says never include footnote markers in canonical labels. For type designations, I could keep \"CMFxxx\" and \"CMFHCx\" as they're designations, with the footnote marker referenced. The schema for type_designations is just a list of strings. I'll include \"CMFxxx\" and \"CMFHCx\". The footnote [1] explains xxx/x denotes size of the measurement sensor. I'll add footnote to footnotes[] with marker \"[1]\" and text.\n\nAlso tables have `<sup>(◇)</sup>` and `<sup>(◇◇)</sup>` markers for MVD700 and MVD800 — these are likely symbols referring to footnotes possibly on later pages. I could record them as footnote_markers? They aren't defined in this fragment. I could include them as markers on the components. Actually MVD700 and MVD800 are electronics types associated with minimum flow rate values. Let me handle the model matrix.\n\nRecommendation:\n- R 117-1:2007 → id: \"R117-1\", edition: 2007, scheme: \"A\". Hmm, edition as int. R 117-1:2007 — the recommendation is R 117-1, edition year 2007. But the schema says edition (int). Should it be 2007? The certificate number says R117/2007 which is the recommendation year. I'd put id \"R117-1\", edition 2007. Amendment: null/omit. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [\"0.3\", \"0.5\", \"1.0\", \"1.5\"].\n\nTest reports: The table on page 2 lists reports grouped under three headings: \"Measurement sensor\", \"MVD series electronics\", \"5700 electronics\". Each report: id, date, pages, role. Role: these are test/evaluation reports. The text says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". I'll use role \"test_report\" or \"type_evaluation_report\". Since the cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\", these are likely type evaluation reports. I'll use \"test_report\" for these? Hmm. The schema options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. These associated reports are the test reports. I'll mark them all as \"test_report\". Also the documentation folders mentioned on page 3: TC7056-14, TC7057-17, TC8519-4 — \"documentation folders\". These fit test_reports with role \"documentation_file\". Yes, include them.\n\nLet me list the reports:\n\nMeasurement sensor:\n- CVN/201269, 10 July 2002, 80 pages — note: page 2 first row says \"CVN/201269\" but in MVD series it's \"CVN-201269\". I'll keep as written.\n- CVN-410178-01, 21 December 2005, 6\n- CVN-410178-02, 21 December 2005, 8\n- CVN-410178-03, 21 December 2005, 8\n- CVN-410178-04, 21 December 2005, 6\n- C-SP/603876, 12 July 2006, 23\n- CPC-802620-1, 11 June 2009, 25\n- CPC-9200041-1, 15 October 2009, 5\n- CPC-9200087-1, 15 October 2009, 6\n- NMi-10200543-1, 11 January 2011, 4\n- NMi-10200543-2, 5 April 2011, 6\n- NMi-2591028-01, 30 September 2021, 9\n- NMi-3073006-01, 14 November 2022, 10\n- NMi-3073006-02, 23 November 2022, 10\n- NMi-3800938-01, 11 September 2024, 11\n\nMVD series electronics:\n- CVN-201269, 10 July 2002, 80\n- CPC-307228-1, 21 February 2005, 35\n- CPC-607580-1, 26 April 2007, 32\n- CPC-610406-2, 29 January 2008, 31\n- CPC-710466-1, 19 November 2008, 32\n- NMi-11200214-01, 17 May 2011, 13\n- NMi-11200345-02, 20 October 2011, 8\n- NMi-1901208-01, 5 July 2018, 30\n\n5700 electronics:\n- NMi-14200115-01, 4 December 2015, 68\n- NMi-14200115-02, 4 December 2015, 52\n- NMi-14200115-06, 22 April 2016, 21\n- NMi-15200770-01, 4 February 2016, 9\n- NMi-2571596-01, 30 September 2021, 38\n\nDates: ISO format? The schema doesn't specify format. I'll use ISO 8601: \"2002-07-10\" etc. For date_issued: \"2024-09-11\".\n\nComponents: The cert names sub-assemblies: measurement sensor (CMF/CMFHC series), MVD series electronics (MVD700, MVD800), 5700 electronics. The type is a measurement transducer composed of sensor + electronics. I could add components:\n- role \"measurement sensor\", type_designations: [\"CMFxxx\", \"CMFHCx\"]... Actually the sensor types are CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y (y presumably denotes something). Hmm — the table lists sensor types CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y. And footnote [1] says xxx or x denotes the size. So CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMF100 are sizes. \n\nModel family: The models here are the sensor sizes: CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMF100. Attributes vary per sensor size: maximum flow rate, minimum flow rate (per electronics and accuracy class), minimum measured quantity, maximum pressure. This is a model matrix with sensors as models, and config axis of electronics (MVD700 vs MVD800) and accuracy class.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"CMF series\" (Table 1 says \"General characteristics applicable to the CMF series of measurement sensors\"). Could be \"CMFxxx / CMFHCx\". I'll use \"CMF series\".\n- models: model_id: \"CMF010\", \"CMF025\", \"CMF050\", \"CMF100\". But type designations are CMF010y etc. with y being something (maybe process connection or material variant?). The footnote says \"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor\". CMFxxx → CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMF100 (three digits); CMFHCx → CMFHC1? Hmm. Actually CMFHCx probably has a single digit/letter size. The table header shows \"CMF010y\", \"CMF025y\", \"CMF050y\", \"CMF100y\" — y is part of the designation. I'll use model_id like \"CMF010y\", \"CMF025y\", \"CMF050y\", \"CMF100y\" to stay faithful to the table.\n\ncharacteristics:\n\ntype_level (Table 1 general characteristics):\n- density_range: {value: {min: 400, max: 1100}, unit_symbol: \"kg/m³\"}\n- maximum_viscosity: {value: 1080, unit_symbol: \"mm²/s\"} — note \"(cSt)\" also mentioned. I'll add unit note or just unit_symbol \"mm²/s (cSt)\". Better: unit_symbol \"mm²/s\". The text: \"1080 mm²/s (cSt) under actual operating conditions\". Hmm, mm²/s equals cSt. unit_symbol: \"mm²/s (cSt)\" is acceptable? I'll use \"mm²/s\".\n- accuracy_class: {value: [\"0.3\",\"0.5\",\"1.0\",\"1.5\"]}? The structured value should be single. Accuracy classes go in recommendation.accuracy_classes. But could also record. I'll include accuracy_classes in recommendation only. Actually characteristics type_level could include it, but it's redundant. Skip.\n- environmental_classes: {value: \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\"} — string. unit null.\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- intended_fluids or measured_liquids: {value: \"Oil and oil products, alcohol, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure, and cryogenic liquids\"} — string.\n\nAlso liquid temperature ranges by accuracy class (config axis = accuracy class):\n- temperature_range_liquid_mass_measurement: classes 0.3/0.5/1.0: -10 … +250 °C; class 1.5: -200 … +50 °C.\n- temperature_range_liquid_density_volume_measurement: -10 … +150 °C (classes 0.3-1.0; the 1.5 column is empty in the table).\n\nThese vary by accuracy class → config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\". Values follow {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute \"liquid_temperature_range_mass_measurement\", axis \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"0.3; 0.5; 1.0\", value: {min:-10,max:250}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}, {condition:\"1.5\", value:{min:-200,max:50}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}]\n- attribute \"liquid_temperature_range_density_volume_measurement\", axis \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"0.3; 0.5; 1.0\", value: {min:-10,max:150}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}]\n\nmodel_level (Table 2), models CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y:\n\n- maximum_flow_rate, unit kg/min: CMF010y: 1.8 (comma decimal 1,8 → 1.8), CMF025y: 36, CMF050y: 110, CMF100y: 450.\n- minimum_measured_quantity, unit kg: 0.05, 0.5, 5, 10.\n- maximum_pressure, unit bar(g): 25 all.\n- minimum_flow_rate: this varies by accuracy class AND electronics AND sensor. This is complex. Options: emit as model_level entries with combined attribute naming, or as config_level with axis combining electronics and accuracy class.\n\nThe structure: For MVD700:\n- Accuracy class 0.3: CMF010y: 0.033 (H,L,M) / 0.067 (P); CMF025y: 0.46; CMF050y: 2.72; CMF100y: 11.4\n- Accuracy class 0.5; 1.0; 1.5: CMF010y: 0.017 (H,L,M) / 0.033 (P); CMF025y: 0.23; CMF050y: 1.36; CMF100y: 5.7\n\nFor MVD800:\n- Accuracy class 0.3: 0.033 (H,L,M) / 0.067 (P); 0.45; 1.3; 7.83\n- Accuracy class 0.5: 0.017 (H,L,M) / 0.033 (P); 0.23; 0.65; 3.92\n- Accuracy class 1.0; 1.5: 0.017 (H,L,M) / 0.033 (P); 0.18; 0.57; 2.3\n\nThe H, L, M, P superscripts — footnote markers not defined in this fragment (probably defined on later pages, e.g., H = horizontal, etc.). I'll keep them as footnote_markers? They're not defined here. The rules say extract footnote definitions; markers can be recorded even if definition unknown? I'll include footnote_markers [\"H, L, M\"] and [\"P\"]... Hmm, but rule 1 says never include footnote markers in labels. Values may have footnote_markers. Since the markers are superscripts in the cell values, I could include them as footnote_markers: [\"H\",\"L\",\"M\"]. But they aren't defined in this fragment. I'll include them since they appear as superscripts.\n\nActually the ◇ symbols on MVD700/MVD800 — these are likely footnote markers referring to something (maybe \"corresponding to specific MVD versions\"). I'll include \"(◇)\" as footnote marker on component entries? Might be too speculative. The components section: characteristics{} is free. I could record electronics type info.\n\nFor minimum flow rate, the cleanest representation:\n\nmodel_level entries with attribute names that encode electronics and class:\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_accuracy_class_0.3 — attribute names should be canonical snake_case. Like \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0_3\".\n\nAlternatively use config_level with axis \"electronics and accuracy class\". Rule 5 says config axes when attributes vary by configuration (not model). Here min flow rate varies by sensor model AND electronics config AND accuracy class. The model matrix handles the model dimension; the config axis handles electronics+class. So:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate\", axis: \"electronics/accuracy_class\" — but config values only have {condition, value}, no model dimension. Hmm, but each config_level entry can have values per condition... and the model dimension is lost.\n\nAlternative: combine model in condition. That gets messy.\n\nBetter approach: emit config_level entries with conditions like \"MVD700, accuracy class 0.3\" and values per model? The schema config_level: {attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}}. There's no model field. Hmm.\n\nMaybe I can put config_level entries per attribute with axis \"electronics\" and conditions \"MVD700 | accuracy class 0.3\" etc., and include model in... no.\n\nAlternative approach: put minimum flow rate in model_level with attribute names that encode the electronics and class:\n\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0_3\", unit kg/min, values per model.\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0_5_1_0_1_5\"\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd800_class_0_3\"\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd800_class_0_5\"\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd800_class_1_0_1_5\"\n\nThis preserves all data and the model dimension. But CMF010y values are dual: \"0,033 H,L,M / 0,067 P\". The H/L/M vs P likely denote mounting/orientation or application (H horizontal, L, M, P...?). Since footnote markers are superscripts on the numbers, the two numbers correspond to different footnote groups. So value could be a string \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\"? Rule 2 wants numbers. Ranges → min/max. This isn't a range; it's two conditional values. Hmm.\n\nI could represent as value string \"0.033 (H, L, M); 0.067 (P)\" with footnote markers. Or make separate model_level entries: \"minimum_flow_rate_hlm\" and \"minimum_flow_rate_p\"? But H/L/M/P meaning is unknown in this fragment.\n\nSimplest faithful: value: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\" as a string with decimal conversion. But rules prefer numbers... They say numbers not strings for numeric values; but compound values can be strings (formulas). A dual conditional value is best as a string preserving both numbers. I'll use string: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\" and footnote_markers [\"H\",\"L\",\"M\",\"P\"]? Hmm, maybe better: value: {\"hlm\": 0.033, \"p\": 0.067}? That's non-standard.\n\nAlternatively, emit two model_level attributes:\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_class_X_mvd700\" with values as numbers where single, and for CMF010y where dual, use the HLM value as main and mention P via footnote? Risky.\n\nI think the most reasonable within schema constraints: model_level entries with value strings for the dual ones: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\". Numbers elsewhere. And footnote_markers: [\"H, L, M\", \"P\"] — markers array of strings. Actually footnote_markers is meant for footnote definitions. Since these are superscript letter markers, I'll record them.\n\nHmm wait — let me reconsider. The H, L, M, P superscripts likely refer to footnotes that explain those values apply to certain conditions. Given the fragment doesn't define them, keeping them attached as footnote markers preserves the info. \n\nActually, maybe I should treat H/L/M/P as orientation codes: In Micro Motion CMF sensors, minimum flow rate differs per... Actually I recall in NMi certificates for Emerson CMF sensors, H = horizontal, V = vertical... The letters H, L, M might be accuracy designations... I shouldn't guess. Keep as markers.\n\nLet me now build the model_level for minimum_flow_rate. I'll name attributes:\n\n\"minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_accurac_class_0_3\" — typo careful: \"minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0_3\".\n\nValues:\nMVD700, class 0.3:\n- CMF010y: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\" markers [\"H, L, M\", \"P\"]? I'll do footnote_markers: [\"H\",\"L\",\"M\",\"P\"]. Hmm, cleaner: [\"H, L, M\", \"P\"] as two marker groups matching the two numbers. I'll go with that.\n\nActually, footnote_markers entries conventionally look like \"1)\". Multi-letter groups as separate strings is fine.\n\n- CMF025y: 0.46\n- CMF050y: 2.72\n- CMF100y: 11.4\n\nMVD700, class 0.5/1.0/1.5:\n- CMF010y: \"0.017 (H, L, M) / 0.033 (P)\"\n- CMF025y: 0.23\n- CMF050y: 1.36\n- CMF100y: 5.7\n\nMVD800, class 0.3:\n- CMF010y: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\"\n- CMF025y: 0.45\n- CMF050y: 1.3\n- CMF100y: 7.83\n\nMVD800, class 0.5:\n- CMF010y: \"0.017 (H, L, M) / 0.033 (P)\"\n- CMF025y: 0.23\n- CMF050y: 0.65\n- CMF100y: 3.92\n\nMVD800, class 1.0/1.5:\n- CMF010y: \"0.017 (H, L, M) / 0.033 (P)\"\n- CMF025y: 0.18\n- CMF050y: 0.57\n- CMF100y: 2.3\n\nComponents:\n- role: \"measurement sensor\", type_designations: [\"CMFxxx\", \"CMFHCx\"], maybe characteristics: documentation_folder \"TC7056-14\".\n- role: \"electronics\", type_designations: [\"MVD700\", \"MVD800\"], alternatives null? The cert doesn't say OR; both are covered. documentation TC7057-17. series \"MVD series electronics\".\n- role: \"electronics\", type_designations: [\"5700\"], documentation TC8519-4.\n\nThe ◇ markers on MVD700 (◇) and MVD800 (◇◇) — these are probably footnote markers defined later. I could add footnote_markers to the component characteristics? The component entry has characteristics{} free-form. I could skip.\n\nmatrix_tables: The tables don't strictly need matrix_tables since model_level covers them. But Table 2 is a multi-dim table. I could add a matrix_table for Table 2 preserving it. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. Model level covers the flow rates; min measured quantity and max pressure also in model_level. I think I'll add one matrix_table for \"Table 2 – Specific characteristics of the CMF series of measurement sensors\" to be safe and preserve the (◇) markers. That's reasonable. But it might be redundant. The R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level. I'll include matrix_tables for Table 2, with rows per sensor type and columns. The min flow rate rows have row-span electronics condition. Rows as objects:\n\n{\n \"name\": \"Table 2 – Specific characteristics of the CMF series of measurement sensors\",\n \"columns\": [\"sensor_type\", \"electronics\", \"accuracy_class\", \"maximum_flow_rate\", \"minimum_flow_rate\", \"minimum_measured_quantity\", \"maximum_pressure\"],\n \"column_units\": [null, null, null, \"kg/min\", \"kg/min\", \"kg\", \"bar(g)\"],\n \"rows\": [\n   {\"sensor_type\": \"CMF010y\", \"electronics\": \"MVD700\", \"accuracy_class\": \"0.3\", \"maximum_flow_rate\": 1.8, \"minimum_flow_rate\": \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\", \"minimum_measured_quantity\": 0.05, \"maximum_pressure\": 25},\n   ...\n ]\n}\n\nHmm this is getting big. Rows: sensor × electronics × class combos: MVD700: 2 class rows; MVD800: 3 class rows → 5 rows × 4 sensors = 20 rows. That's a lot but fine.\n\nActually, maybe skip matrix_tables since model_level captures everything, and the fragment's table structure is fully represented. The R137 example includes both because that table is inherently 2-D. Here I've decomposed into model_level attributes. I think including matrix_tables is optional. To keep output manageable and faithful, I'll include the matrix table since it preserves the MVD700/MVD800 ◇ markers and grouping. Hmm. Let me include it — it's valuable.\n\nActually wait, I need to be careful about size. The response must be JSON only. 20 rows is fine.\n\nLet me decide model_ids: \"CMF010y\", \"CMF025y\", \"CMF050y\", \"CMF100y\". But CMFHCx series also exists. The table only covers CMF series. The type designations list: CMFxxx, CMFHCx. Models covered in the table are CMF series with y suffix. y likely denotes a variant. I'll use model_ids from the table header.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"CMF series\" — Table 1 title mentions \"CMF series of measurement sensors\". Also CMFHC series mentioned. I'll set family_name: \"CMF / CMFHC series\" or null. Hmm. Table 1: \"General characteristics applicable to the CMF series of measurement sensors\". \"Further characteristics of the CMF and CMFHC series measurement sensor\". I'll use family_name \"CMF series\" — but CMFHC included... I'll say \"CMF and CMFHC series\". Fine.\n\nFootnotes:\n- marker \"[1]\": \"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"\n\nThe ◇ footnotes not defined here; skip.\n\nRevision history: \"revision 3\" appears in number; no revision history table in this fragment. The number string includes \"revision 3\" — the certificate number field should probably be \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" and revision \"3\"? Schema has